WEBVTT

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.358
Meet MXI RoboICL.

00:00:04.638 --> 00:00:08.137
Our team builds on learning through context, with geometry

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:10.770
that helps robots act more precisely.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.955
RoboICL originally reserved twelve slots for one expert

00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:19.710
demonstration and twelve for the current episode.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.110
MXI moves six of those slots to the demonstration. The

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.154
model sees eighteen expert windows and up to six online

00:00:28.154 --> 00:00:29.110
interactions.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.292
Each window pairs two observations with the actions in

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:36.906
between. The receipt tells the model how much of the

00:00:36.906 --> 00:00:37.670
action ran.

00:00:37.928 --> 00:00:40.598
The current episode follows the same format.

00:00:40.878 --> 00:00:44.490
Selected earlier interactions and the latest result join the

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.735
demonstration in the model’s context.

00:00:47.028 --> 00:00:50.210
The model decides, acts, and observes again.

00:00:50.503 --> 00:00:51.786
Its weights stay frozen.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.395
Here are two wrist images from Fill Pen Holder. In the

00:00:55.395 --> 00:00:59.064
demonstration, the right hand holds the purple pen. In the

00:00:59.064 --> 00:01:01.910
current episode, the left hand approaches it.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.365
Look at the red cross. The pen is beside it in the current

00:01:06.365 --> 00:01:09.974
view. The cyan edges show where the finger pads will

00:01:09.974 --> 00:01:10.390
close.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.326
Later in the episode, the purple pen sits between the

00:01:14.326 --> 00:01:18.030
fingers. Both views use the same marks, even though they

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.750
come from different hands.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.484
Each wrist image keeps the camera pose from its own

00:01:23.484 --> 00:01:27.176
observation. That lets the policy relate the image to the

00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:28.990
coordinates it uses to move.

00:01:29.248 --> 00:01:31.356
Watch the target approach the crosshair.

00:01:31.636 --> 00:01:32.256
Align.

00:01:32.548 --> 00:01:33.218
Close.

00:01:33.498 --> 00:01:34.006
Lift.

00:01:34.286 --> 00:01:38.773
Geometry makes the offset visible; the moving object reveals

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:39.456
the result.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.056
For an optional position estimate, the model picks the

00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:46.515
same feature in two views. Here it selected a corner of

00:01:46.515 --> 00:01:47.710
the pen holder rim.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.067
The rays meet at an angle of twenty seven degrees. Their

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:55.995
closest separation is just over a tenth of a millimetre.

00:01:55.995 --> 00:01:58.870
That measures agreement between the rays.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.652
A target is only the beginning.

00:02:01.932 --> 00:02:04.052
The arm still needs a route it can execute.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.865
Here, the first preview fails inverse kinematics.

00:02:08.158 --> 00:02:09.340
No motion is sent.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.165
A revised plan passes.

00:02:11.445 --> 00:02:14.540
Now watch a translucent preview of the recorded arm motion:

00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:18.728
approach, grasp, lift, transfer, and pour.

00:02:19.008 --> 00:02:21.178
Joint targets change along the route.

00:02:21.458 --> 00:02:25.370
This preview explains the accepted sequence; it is not a second

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.515
physical robot.

00:02:26.795 --> 00:02:30.852
The overlay clears, and the recorded arm completes the task.

00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:32.990
The episode scores one hundred.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.051
On ten matched pouring layouts, adding the route tools

00:02:39.051 --> 00:02:42.981
raised the mean score from seventy to one hundred. Three

00:02:42.981 --> 00:02:46.070
layouts improved and seven kept full scores.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.959
On twenty two tasks shared with the original report, the

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:53.232
category balanced score rose from fifty two point two

00:02:53.232 --> 00:02:55.270
seven to eighty point five eight.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.996
Across all thirty four tasks in four categories, MXI

00:02:59.996 --> 00:03:04.705
scored seventy five point nine three. Including the eight

00:03:04.705 --> 00:03:08.670
Open tasks gives seventy five point three seven.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.253
Twenty two of the thirty four task configurations supplied

00:03:13.253 --> 00:03:17.119
no extra written task note. The model still received a

00:03:17.119 --> 00:03:20.270
demonstration and its own execution history.

00:03:44.378 --> 00:03:47.498
MXI RoboICL.

00:03:47.791 --> 00:03:49.673
An MXI team upgrade.

00:03:49.978 --> 00:03:50.861
Thank you for watching.
